## Why it exists

Every web interface, from a hand-written landing page to a React app with a million lines, ends up as three things the browser understands: **HTML** (what is on the page), **CSS** (how it looks) and **JavaScript** (what it does). Frameworks, compilers and design tools all produce these three. If you know them from the ground up, framework behaviour stops being magic, bugs become findable in the browser's DevTools, and you can judge whether a tool is helping or hiding something.

**Mental model:** the browser downloads HTML and builds a tree of objects called the **DOM** (Document Object Model). CSS rules are matched against that tree to decide how every node looks. JavaScript reads and changes the tree, and the browser re-renders. Three languages, three jobs: **structure, presentation, behaviour**. Keep each job in its own language and the page stays easy to change.

{{dg:0}}

## How it works

### HTML: structure and meaning

An HTML file is text with **elements** written as tags (`<p>text</p>`), **attributes** (`<a href="/about">`) and a nested structure. Void elements such as `<img>`, `<input>` and `<br>` have no closing tag. A minimal modern page:

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    <title>Hello, web</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <h1>Hello, web</h1>
    <p>This is a <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/">link</a>.</p>
  </body>
</html>
```

`<!doctype html>` selects standards mode; `lang` helps screen readers and translation; `charset` avoids garbled text; the `viewport` tag makes layouts work on phones. Choose elements for **meaning**, not looks: that is **semantic HTML**.

- Landmarks: `<header>`, `<nav>`, `<main>` (one per page), `<section>`, `<article>`, `<aside>`, `<footer>`. Screen-reader users jump between them.
- Headings `<h1>` to `<h6>` form an outline: one `h1`, no skipped levels. Style size with CSS, not by picking a smaller heading.
- Links (`<a href>`) **navigate**; buttons (`<button>`) **do something**. A clickable `<div>` has no keyboard support, no focus and no role. Use the real element.
- Lists (`<ul>`, `<ol>`, `<li>`), tables (`<table>` for tabular data only, with `<th scope>`), `<time datetime>`, `<figure>` and `<figcaption>`.
- Images: `<img src alt width height>`. `alt` describes the image's purpose (empty `alt=""` for decoration), and `width` and `height` reserve space so the page does not jump while loading. `loading="lazy"` defers off-screen images.

Forms are where HTML does the most work for free:

```html
<form action="/subscribe" method="post">
  <label for="email">Email</label>
  <input id="email" name="email" type="email" required autocomplete="email">

  <label for="plan">Plan</label>
  <select id="plan" name="plan">
    <option value="free">Free</option>
    <option value="pro">Pro</option>
  </select>

  <label><input type="checkbox" name="news"> Send me news</label>
  <button type="submit">Subscribe</button>
</form>
```

Every control needs a **label** (clicking it focuses the input; screen readers announce it). `name` is the key the server receives. `type="email"`, `required`, `min`, `max`, `pattern` give built-in validation and the right mobile keyboard. Browser validation is a convenience, never security: the server must validate again.

### CSS: selectors, the cascade and the box model

A CSS rule is `selector { property: value; }`. Common selectors:

```css
p { line-height: 1.6; }                 /* type: every <p> */
.card { padding: 1rem; }                /* class: class="card" */
#main-title { font-size: 2rem; }        /* id: id="main-title" (avoid for styling) */
nav a { text-decoration: none; }        /* descendant: any <a> inside <nav> */
ul > li { margin: 0; }                  /* child: direct children only */
input[type="email"] { width: 100%; }    /* attribute */
button:hover, a:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid; }   /* pseudo-classes: state */
```

When several rules match the same element, the **cascade** picks a winner: first by importance (`!important` wins, use it almost never), then by **specificity** (inline style beats ID beats class, attribute and pseudo-class, which beat element selectors), then by **source order** (the later rule wins). Some properties, such as `color` and `font-family`, are **inherited** by children; others, such as `margin` and `border`, are not.

Every element is a rectangular **box**: content, then `padding`, then `border`, then `margin`. By default `width` sets only the content, so adding padding makes the box wider than you asked. Almost every stylesheet starts with:

```css
*, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; }  /* width now includes padding and border */
```

Units: `px` is fixed; `rem` scales with the root font size (and the user's browser setting, which is why it is good for accessibility); `%` is relative to the parent; `vw` and `vh` are relative to the viewport; `ch` is about one digit wide (good for `max-width: 65ch` line lengths). Colors can live in **custom properties** (CSS variables) so a theme is defined once:

```css
:root { --bg: #ffffff; --fg: #1a1a1a; --accent: #0b6bcb; }
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
  :root { --bg: #121212; --fg: #eaeaea; --accent: #6cb2ff; }
}
body { background: var(--bg); color: var(--fg); }
a { color: var(--accent); }
```

### CSS layout: flexbox, grid and responsive design

**Flexbox** lays items out along **one axis** (a row or a column): navigation bars, button groups, centring. **Grid** lays out in **two dimensions**: page layouts, card galleries. Use `gap` for spacing between items instead of margins.

```css
.nav { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; gap: 1rem; }
.nav .spacer { flex: 1; }            /* takes all leftover space */

.cards {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(16rem, 1fr));  /* as many 16rem+ columns as fit */
  gap: 1rem;
}
```

Design **mobile first**: write the single-column layout as the default, then add `@media (min-width: 48rem) { ... }` rules for wider screens. `position: relative` anchors absolutely positioned children; `position: sticky; top: 0` keeps a header in view. Newer features such as container queries and the `:has()` selector are widely available in current browsers, but check caniuse.com or MDN's compatibility tables for your audience.

### JavaScript: the language in 10 minutes

```javascript
const name = "Ada";            // const: cannot be reassigned (the object it holds can still change)
let count = 0;                 // let: reassignable, block scoped. Avoid var.
console.log(`Hello ${name}, count is ${count}`);   // template literal

// Types: number, string, boolean, null, undefined, bigint, symbol, and objects (arrays and functions too)
console.log(typeof 42, typeof "x", typeof null, typeof undefined, typeof [], typeof (() => 1));
console.log(0.1 + 0.2 === 0.3, 1 === "1", 1 == "1");   // floats are inexact; use === not ==
console.log(Boolean(0), Boolean(""), Boolean([]), Boolean("0"));  // falsy: 0, "", null, undefined, NaN, false

// Functions
function add(a, b = 0) { return a + b; }
const double = (n) => n * 2;           // arrow function

// Arrays and objects
const people = [{ name: "Ada", age: 36 }, { name: "Linus", age: 28 }, { name: "Grace", age: 85 }];
const names = people.map((p) => p.name);
const adults = people.filter((p) => p.age >= 30);
const totalAge = people.reduce((sum, p) => sum + p.age, 0);
console.log(names, adults.length, totalAge);

const { age, ...rest } = people[0];             // destructuring and rest
const older = { ...people[0], age: age + 1 };   // spread makes a changed copy
console.log(older, rest);

// Optional chaining and nullish coalescing
const user = { profile: null };
console.log(user.profile?.email ?? "no email");

// Closures: a function remembers the variables around where it was created
function counter() { let n = 0; return () => ++n; }
const next = counter();
next(); next();
console.log(next());
```

Run it with Node (`node file.mjs`) or paste it into the browser console. Key habits: use `===`, prefer `const`, treat arrays and objects as values you copy rather than mutate, and remember `typeof null` is `"object"` (a historic bug that can never be fixed).

### JavaScript in the browser: DOM, events and async

The browser gives JavaScript a global `document`. Find elements with `document.querySelector("#list")`, read and change them with `.textContent`, `.classList`, `.setAttribute`, create them with `document.createElement`, and react with `addEventListener`.

- **textContent vs innerHTML:** `el.textContent = userText` is always safe. `el.innerHTML = userText` parses HTML and runs it, which is how **cross-site scripting (XSS)** happens. Never put untrusted text in `innerHTML`.
- **Event delegation:** instead of one listener per list item, attach one listener to the parent and check `event.target.closest("button")`. It also works for items added later.
- **Forms:** `new FormData(form)` collects values; call `event.preventDefault()` in a submit handler to stop the page reload.
- **Script loading:** `<script type="module" src="app.js">` is deferred automatically, can use `import` and `export`, and runs in strict mode.
- **Async:** a **Promise** represents a value that arrives later. `async` functions use `await` to wait without blocking the page. `fetch(url)` returns a promise of a `Response`; it only rejects on network failure, so check `response.ok` yourself.

```javascript
import { createServer } from "node:http";

// A tiny local API so this example runs anywhere with Node 18+ (browsers have the same fetch).
const server = createServer((req, res) => {
  if (req.url === "/users/1") {
    res.setHeader("content-type", "application/json");
    res.end(JSON.stringify({ id: 1, name: "Ada" }));
  } else {
    res.statusCode = 404;
    res.end("not found");
  }
}).listen(0);
const base = `http://127.0.0.1:${server.address().port}`;

async function getJson(url) {
  const response = await fetch(url);
  if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status} for ${url}`);   // 404 does NOT reject by itself
  return response.json();
}

try {
  console.log(await getJson(`${base}/users/1`));
  await getJson(`${base}/users/999`);
} catch (err) {
  console.log("caught:", err.message.replace(/:\d+\//, ":PORT/"));
} finally {
  server.close();
}
```

## Worked example

A complete single-file to-do list. It uses semantic HTML, CSS variables, flexbox, event delegation, `textContent` (no XSS), and `localStorage` wrapped in `try/catch` because storage can be unavailable. The state functions are pure so they can be tested without a browser; the DOM wiring comes last.

```html
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title>Todos</title>
  <style>
    *, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; }
    :root { --bg: #fff; --fg: #1a1a1a; --accent: #0b6bcb; --line: #d9d9d9; }
    @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
      :root { --bg: #121212; --fg: #eaeaea; --accent: #6cb2ff; --line: #3a3a3a; }
    }
    body { margin: 0; font: 1rem/1.5 system-ui, sans-serif; background: var(--bg); color: var(--fg); }
    main { max-width: 36rem; margin: 0 auto; padding: 1rem; }
    form, .filters { display: flex; gap: .5rem; margin-block: 1rem; }
    input[type="text"] { flex: 1; padding: .5rem; }
    ul { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; }
    li { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: .5rem; padding: .5rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
    li.done span { text-decoration: line-through; opacity: .6; }
    li span { flex: 1; }
    button { cursor: pointer; padding: .4rem .8rem; }
    .visually-hidden { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; overflow: hidden; clip-path: inset(50%); white-space: nowrap; }
    button:focus-visible, input:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 2px; }
    button[aria-pressed="true"] { background: var(--accent); color: var(--bg); }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <main>
    <h1>Todos</h1>
    <form id="add">
      <label for="text" class="visually-hidden">New todo</label>
      <input id="text" type="text" placeholder="What needs doing?" required>
      <button type="submit">Add</button>
    </form>
    <div class="filters" role="group" aria-label="Filter">
      <button data-filter="all" aria-pressed="true">All</button>
      <button data-filter="open" aria-pressed="false">Open</button>
      <button data-filter="done" aria-pressed="false">Done</button>
    </div>
    <ul id="list"></ul>
  </main>
  <script type="module">
    // ---- pure state functions (no DOM) ----
    const addTodo = (list, text) =>
      text.trim() ? [...list, { id: Math.max(0, ...list.map((t) => t.id)) + 1, text: text.trim(), done: false }] : list;
    const toggle = (list, id) => list.map((t) => (t.id === id ? { ...t, done: !t.done } : t));
    const remove = (list, id) => list.filter((t) => t.id !== id);
    const visible = (list, filter) =>
      filter === "all" ? list : list.filter((t) => (filter === "done" ? t.done : !t.done));

    // ---- DOM wiring ----
    const KEY = "todos-v1";
    let todos = [];
    let filter = "all";
    try { todos = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(KEY) ?? "[]"); } catch { todos = []; }
    const save = () => { try { localStorage.setItem(KEY, JSON.stringify(todos)); } catch { /* private mode: ignore */ } };

    const list = document.querySelector("#list");
    function render() {
      list.replaceChildren(...visible(todos, filter).map((t) => {
        const li = document.createElement("li");
        li.dataset.id = t.id;
        li.classList.toggle("done", t.done);
        const span = document.createElement("span");
        span.textContent = t.text;                 // textContent: safe for untrusted text
        const doneBtn = Object.assign(document.createElement("button"), { textContent: t.done ? "Undo" : "Done" });
        doneBtn.dataset.action = "toggle";
        const delBtn = Object.assign(document.createElement("button"), { textContent: "Delete" });
        delBtn.dataset.action = "remove";
        li.append(span, doneBtn, delBtn);
        return li;
      }));
      save();
    }

    document.querySelector("#add").addEventListener("submit", (e) => {
      e.preventDefault();
      const input = e.target.elements.text;
      todos = addTodo(todos, input.value);
      input.value = "";
      render();
    });

    list.addEventListener("click", (e) => {            // one listener for every row (delegation)
      const btn = e.target.closest("button[data-action]");
      if (!btn) return;
      const id = Number(btn.closest("li").dataset.id);
      todos = btn.dataset.action === "toggle" ? toggle(todos, id) : remove(todos, id);
      render();
    });

    document.querySelector(".filters").addEventListener("click", (e) => {
      const btn = e.target.closest("button[data-filter]");
      if (!btn) return;
      filter = btn.dataset.filter;
      document.querySelectorAll(".filters button").forEach((b) => b.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(b === btn)));
      render();
    });

    render();
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

Save it as `todos.html` and open it in a browser; no server or build step is needed. Things to try in DevTools: inspect the **Elements** panel to see the live DOM, toggle styles in the **Styles** pane to watch the cascade, break on a click in **Sources**, watch **Application > Local Storage**, and throttle the network in **Network**. The `visually-hidden` class (defined in the stylesheet above) hides the label visually but keeps it readable to screen readers, a standard accessibility pattern.

## In real products

- **React, Vue and Svelte** produce DOM operations for you, but props become attributes, `className` becomes `class`, events are the same events, and CSS is still CSS. A component library is semantic HTML plus styles plus behaviour packaged together.
- **Design systems** are CSS custom properties (tokens) and a set of components built on flexbox and grid; dark mode is the `prefers-color-scheme` pattern above.
- **Accessibility** is mostly semantic HTML done right: real buttons, labelled inputs, visible focus, good contrast. Regulations such as the European Accessibility Act and the ADA make this a legal requirement for many products, not only a nicety.
- **Performance** (see the web performance lesson) is largely about HTML, CSS and JS size and order: the browser cannot paint until it has the CSS, and a large synchronous script blocks interaction.
- **Progressive enhancement:** a plain HTML form that posts to the server still works if JavaScript fails; JavaScript then upgrades it. Frameworks such as Next.js and Remix rediscovered this pattern.

> [!pitfall] Using `<div onclick>` as a button. It cannot be focused with the keyboard, is not announced as a button, and ignores Enter and Space. Use `<button>`.

> [!pitfall] Forgetting `box-sizing: border-box`, then wondering why `width: 100%` plus padding overflows the screen.

> [!pitfall] Fighting specificity with `!important` or long selector chains. Keep selectors flat (one class), and the later-rule-wins order does the rest.

> [!pitfall] Checking only `await fetch(...)` for errors. A 404 or 500 response resolves normally; test `response.ok`. Also `==` coerces types (`0 == ""` is true); use `===`.

> [!tip] Open DevTools first, always. The Elements, Network and Console panels answer most "why does it look or behave like this" questions in seconds, and `console.table(array)` prints arrays of objects readably.

> [!tip] Build layouts with intrinsic sizing (`max-width: 65ch`, `minmax()`, `flex-wrap`, `clamp()`) before reaching for many media queries. Test at 320 px wide, with browser zoom at 200 percent, and with only the keyboard.

> [!cost] Every kilobyte of JavaScript costs download, parse and execute time, and phones pay the most. A page that needs no JS to show its content is fastest. Measure with Lighthouse or the DevTools Performance panel before adding libraries.

> [!security] Treat everything from users, URLs and APIs as untrusted: write it with `textContent`, never `innerHTML`; never put secrets or API keys in front-end code (anyone can read it); and remember `localStorage` is readable by any script on the page, so do not store session tokens there. Add a Content Security Policy for real apps.

> [!prod] Production pages add: a CSP header, `rel="noopener"` on `target="_blank"` links, hashed asset filenames with long cache lifetimes, compressed responses, `lang` and `alt` text, error reporting for front-end exceptions, and automated accessibility checks (axe) in CI.

> [!version] Verified 2026-10-21 in the sandbox: the HTML in this lesson was parsed with Python's `html.parser` and checked for balanced tags, the full to-do page's script was syntax-checked with `node --check`, the plain JavaScript blocks and the local `fetch` example were executed on Node 22.22.2, and the to-do page's four pure state functions were run in Node against assertions. NOT verified: the CSS (only brace balance was checked), anything that touches the DOM, `localStorage` or the layout (the sandbox has no browser), accessibility behaviour with real screen readers, and browser support claims for container queries and `:has()`, which are stated from general knowledge. Open the page in a real browser to confirm it works for you.

