## Why it exists

Updating a web page by hand (find the node, change its text, remember to undo it later) stops scaling the moment two things can change the same piece of UI. React flips the problem: you describe **what the UI should look like for the current data**, and React works out the DOM changes.

**Mental model:** a component is a pure function from `(props, state)` to a description of UI (JSX). React calls it, compares the new description with the previous one (*reconciliation*), and applies only the difference to the DOM. Rendering is not the same as touching the DOM: a component can render many times and change nothing on screen. Side effects (network calls, subscriptions, timers) do not belong in the render itself; they live in event handlers and effects.

{{dg:0}}

## How it works

**Two phases.** *Render*: React calls your components to compute the next UI; this must be pure and may be repeated or thrown away. *Commit*: React updates the DOM, then runs effects. Anything that must happen "because the screen changed" goes in an effect; anything that happens "because the user clicked" goes in a handler.

**State is a snapshot.** `const [count, setCount] = useState(0)` gives you the value *for this render*. `setCount(count + 1)` three times in one handler adds 1, not 3, because `count` is the same snapshot each time. Use the updater form `setCount(c => c + 1)` when the next value depends on the previous one. State belongs to a component instance at a position in the tree; change the component's **key** and React throws the state away and starts fresh.

**Derive, do not duplicate.** If a value can be computed from props or state during render (a filtered list, a total, a full name), compute it there. Storing it in more state creates two sources of truth that drift apart.

**Effects synchronize with the outside world.** `useEffect(() => { ...; return cleanup }, [deps])` runs after commit and re-runs when a dependency changes; the cleanup runs before the next run and on unmount. In development, React's Strict Mode deliberately mounts, unmounts and re-mounts every component once to prove your cleanup is correct. If an effect breaks under that double run, it has a bug, not Strict Mode. Do not use effects for things you can compute during render or do in an event handler.

**Refs.** `useRef` holds a mutable value that does not trigger a re-render: DOM nodes, timer ids, the previous value. Never read or write `ref.current` during render.

**Stale closures.** Every render creates new handler and effect functions that close over *that render's* values. A `setInterval` created once with an empty dependency list keeps seeing the first render's state forever. Fixes: the updater form, correct dependencies, or `useEffectEvent` (stable in React 19.2) for effect code that should read the latest values without re-triggering the effect.

**Lists and keys.** Give each list item a **stable, unique key** from your data (a database id), not the array index. Index keys make React attach state (typed text, focus, animations) to the wrong row when the list reorders.

**Controlled inputs.** An input whose value comes from state (`value` + `onChange`) is controlled. Uncontrolled inputs (read through a form's `FormData` or a ref) are simpler for plain forms and are what React 19 Actions use.

**Context** passes a value deep without prop drilling, but every consumer re-renders when the value changes. Good for low-churn values (theme, current user, locale); poor for fast-changing state.

**React 19 data-mutation primitives.** `<form action={fn}>` accepts a function, so React manages the pending state for you. `useActionState(fn, initial)` returns `[state, formAction, isPending]`. `useOptimistic` shows the expected result immediately and reverts if the action fails. `useFormStatus` lets a nested button read the parent form's pending state. `use(promise)` reads a promise or context during render, suspending until it resolves. `ref` is now an ordinary prop on function components, so `forwardRef` is no longer needed in new code.

**Transitions.** `useTransition` marks a state update as non-urgent so typing stays responsive while a heavy re-render happens in the background; `useDeferredValue` does the same for a value.

**Memoization is now the compiler's job.** React Compiler 1.0 reached stable in October 2025. It is a build-time tool (a Babel plugin, with integrations for Vite, Next.js and others) that analyzes components and hooks and inserts memoization automatically. Write straightforward code, enable the compiler, and reach for `useMemo`, `useCallback` and `memo` only when profiling shows a problem or you need a stable identity as an effect dependency. It works with React 17 and up (with a small runtime package on 17 and 18), and it relies on you following the Rules of React, which its ESLint rules (in `eslint-plugin-react-hooks`) check.

**Error boundaries.** A render error unmounts the whole tree unless an error boundary catches it. They are still class components (or the `react-error-boundary` package). React 19 added root options `onCaughtError` and `onUncaughtError` to report errors to a service like Sentry in one place.

**React 19.2 additions.** `<Activity>` hides part of the UI while preserving its state and pausing its effects (think background tabs and pre-rendered screens), and `useEffectEvent` as above. As of the June 2026 release notes I could verify, the latest patch was 19.2.7.

**Server Components.** In frameworks like Next.js, components are Server Components by default and ship no JavaScript for themselves; you opt into the client with `"use client"` only where you need state, effects or browser APIs. The Next.js rendering lesson covers that split.

## Worked example

A data-fetching hook with a *correct* effect: it cancels the in-flight request when the id changes or the component unmounts, so a slow old response can never overwrite a newer one.

```tsx
import { useEffect, useState } from "react";

type User = { id: string; name: string };
type Load<T> =
  | { kind: "loading" }
  | { kind: "ok"; data: T }
  | { kind: "error"; message: string };

export function useUser(id: string): Load<User> {
  const [state, setState] = useState<Load<User>>({ kind: "loading" });

  useEffect(() => {
    const ctrl = new AbortController();
    setState({ kind: "loading" });

    fetch(`/api/users/${encodeURIComponent(id)}`, { signal: ctrl.signal })
      .then((r) => {
        if (!r.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${r.status}`);
        return r.json() as Promise<User>;       // validate with a schema in real code
      })
      .then((data) => setState({ kind: "ok", data }))
      .catch((e: unknown) => {
        if (e instanceof DOMException && e.name === "AbortError") return; // expected on cleanup
        setState({ kind: "error", message: e instanceof Error ? e.message : "Failed" });
      });

    return () => ctrl.abort();                  // cleanup: runs before the next effect and on unmount
  }, [id]);

  return state;
}
```

A form using React 19 Actions: no `onSubmit`, no manual `isSubmitting` state.

```tsx
"use client";
import { useActionState } from "react";

type FormState = { error?: string; ok?: boolean };

async function addTodo(_prev: FormState, formData: FormData): Promise<FormState> {
  const title = String(formData.get("title") ?? "").trim();
  if (!title) return { error: "Title is required" };

  const res = await fetch("/api/todos", {
    method: "POST",
    headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
    body: JSON.stringify({ title }),
  });
  return res.ok ? { ok: true } : { error: "Could not save, try again" };
}

export function AddTodo() {
  const [state, formAction, pending] = useActionState(addTodo, {});
  return (
    <form action={formAction}>
      <label>
        Title <input name="title" disabled={pending} />
      </label>
      <button disabled={pending}>{pending ? "Saving…" : "Add"}</button>
      {state.error && <p role="alert">{state.error}</p>}
    </form>
  );
}
```

Optimistic UI on top of a list: the new item appears instantly, and React rolls it back automatically if the action throws.

```tsx
import { useOptimistic } from "react";

type Todo = { id: string; title: string; pending?: boolean };

export function TodoList({ todos, save }: { todos: Todo[]; save: (title: string) => Promise<void> }) {
  const [shown, addOptimistic] = useOptimistic(todos, (current, title: string) => [
    ...current,
    { id: `tmp-${current.length}`, title, pending: true },
  ]);

  async function action(formData: FormData) {
    const title = String(formData.get("title") ?? "").trim();
    if (!title) return;
    addOptimistic(title);       // shown immediately
    await save(title);          // if this throws, the optimistic item disappears
  }

  return (
    <>
      <ul>
        {shown.map((t) => (
          <li key={t.id} style={{ opacity: t.pending ? 0.5 : 1 }}>{t.title}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <form action={action}>
        <input name="title" />
        <button>Add</button>
      </form>
    </>
  );
}
```

