## In real products

- **Next.js App Router** composes Server Components for data and layout with small client components for interactivity; **Vite + React Router** powers client-rendered apps and dashboards; **React Native** uses the same component model on mobile.
- **Server state belongs to a cache library,** not to `useState` plus `useEffect`. TanStack Query (or SWR, or a framework's loaders) gives you caching, deduplication, retries, background refresh and invalidation, which is what hand-written fetch effects keep re-inventing badly.
- **Forms:** React 19 Actions for simple cases; `react-hook-form` with a Zod resolver for complex validation, sharing one schema with the server.
- **Component libraries:** shadcn/ui and Radix for accessible primitives; Storybook for documenting components in isolation.
- **Production monitoring:** Sentry's React SDK plus an error boundary per feature area, so one broken widget does not blank the page.

| State lives in | Use it for | Cost / trap |
|---|---|---|
| `useState` in one component | local UI state (open/closed, input draft) | lifting up when siblings need it |
| URL (search params, route) | filters, tabs, pagination, anything shareable | must parse and validate on read |
| Context | theme, locale, current user | all consumers re-render on any change |
| Server-state cache (TanStack Query) | data owned by the backend | cache keys and invalidation to design |
| External store (Zustand, Redux Toolkit) | complex client-only state shared widely | boilerplate; easy to over-use |
| Form `FormData` (Actions) | submitted values | no live validation without extra code |

> [!pitfall] Using an effect to fetch data without cleanup creates race conditions: responses arrive out of order and an old one overwrites the new. Abort in cleanup, or (better) use a data library or framework loader.

> [!pitfall] Index keys (`key={i}`) on lists that reorder, filter or insert cause state to jump between rows. Use a stable id from the data.

> [!pitfall] Object or array literals created in render and listed as effect dependencies change every render, so the effect re-runs in a loop. Move them out of the component, memoize them, or depend on primitives.

> [!security] `dangerouslySetInnerHTML` and `href={userInput}` are XSS vectors (`javascript:` URLs run code on click). Sanitize HTML with a vetted library, and allow-list URL schemes. React escapes text children by default; do not bypass it casually.

> [!prod] Wrap each feature area in an error boundary that reports to Sentry and shows a fallback, and keep a root-level boundary as the last resort. Add release tags so you can tell which deploy introduced a crash.

> [!cost] Re-render cost usually comes from big lists, context churn and expensive render work, not from "too many components". Profile with React DevTools first; virtualize long lists (TanStack Virtual, react-window); let the compiler memoize before hand-tuning.

> [!tip] Before writing `useEffect`, ask: can I compute this during render, or do it in the event handler that caused it? Most effects people write answer "yes" to one of those.
